WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.400
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.060
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.930
For those of y'all who are new to this,

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.060
this is something that we
started doing a few weeks ago

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.220
as we started seeing
the mass school closures

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.870
and obviously Khan Academy,
we're a not-for-profit

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.150
with a mission of providing
a free world class education

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:23.983
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:26.237
And we realized that as
the schools were closing,

00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:28.410
we wanted to put our resources together

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.820
so they could be as useful as possible

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.329
for you, the student,
the teacher, the parent.

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:35.630
And we also wanted to
provide more supports

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.790
especially since all of us
are now socially distanced,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.580
so that we can feel connected,

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.040
so that we could answer
each other's questions,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.470
so that we can support each other.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.260
And so we've been
running teacher webinars,

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.532
parent webinars and we have
been doing this Daily Homeroom

00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:48.990
which is just a way to connect,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.074
talk about whatever
y'all have on your mind

00:00:51.074 --> 00:00:53.270
and to have interesting guests.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:57.128
Now before we get into the meat
of the homeroom so to speak,

00:00:57.128 --> 00:01:00.760
I will say what I always
say, a reminder that we are

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.593
a not-for-profit.

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:03.660
We are funded with
philanthropic donations.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.280
We were running at a deficit,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.820
even before this COVID crisis hit

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.170
and now our traffic is about 2.5X, 3X

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.670
of what it typically is

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.100
and so our costs have gone more

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.670
and we wanna provide more supports.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.650
So if you are in a position to do so,

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.890
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.591
I do wanna give special thanks
to several corporate partners

00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:25.740
who have stepped up in the last few weeks,

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.910
really in record time,
to help Khan Academy

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:29.743
with this effort.

00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:34.235
Bank of America,
Novartis, Google.org, AT&amp;T

00:01:34.235 --> 00:01:36.477
and of course there's many
other long-time supporters

00:01:36.477 --> 00:01:39.220
but we still need more help.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.820
We're still digging into our reserves

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.590
in order to stay operational.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.650
So now that I've given you my plug,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.855
I wanna introduce our guest.

00:01:46.855 --> 00:01:48.400
You know, we've had a lot of questions

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.350
about all things academic.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.905
How do we keep learning in math?

00:01:51.905 --> 00:01:54.225
How do we keep learning in the humanities

00:01:54.225 --> 00:01:57.550
and how do you keep learning
in foreign language?

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.610
And so I have today, Luis von Ahn,

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.152
a old friend of mine who is
the founder, CEO of Duolingo.

00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:06.970
Many of y'all might be familiar.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:07.860
It's a place, it's an app,

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.950
where you can learn languages for free.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.620
But I don't know, Luis, you're
more qualified than I am.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.730
Tell me about what y'all do at Duolingo.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.560
- Well thank you for having me, Sal.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.084
And thank you for this initiative.

00:02:19.084 --> 00:02:21.120
It's great that you guys are doing it.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.930
Well at Duolingo, I
mean, we teach languages,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.364
that's the main thing we do.

00:02:24.364 --> 00:02:28.128
You can either do it online
in a computer at Duolingo.com

00:02:28.128 --> 00:02:31.453
or you can download an
iPhone app or an Android app.

00:02:31.453 --> 00:02:34.180
It's free to learn, it's
entirely free to learn

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.440
and you can learn as much
as you want for free.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.730
And the other thing is
that we spend a lot of time

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.420
making sure that it's fun to
learn a language with Duolingo.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.520
So it feels a lot like
you're playing a game

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.353
when you're learning on Duolingo.

00:02:46.353 --> 00:02:47.980
We've worked really hard on that.

00:02:49.304 --> 00:02:50.950
- And I can see,

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.310
we're seeing some screenshots
now from Duolingo.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.960
And Luis, I guess, how were
people using Duolingo before?

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.514
Was it being integrated with
your kind of traditional

00:03:00.514 --> 00:03:03.280
you know, high school classes in languages

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.116
or is it mainly adults who
are trying to learn maybe

00:03:05.116 --> 00:03:06.544
for a business trip?

00:03:06.544 --> 00:03:08.900
And how are you seeing the usage change

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.671
since the crisis began?

00:03:10.671 --> 00:03:13.405
- Yeah, I mean, we have a ton of users.

00:03:13.405 --> 00:03:16.563
I mean, we have over 300
million users worldwide.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.320
It's all over the place.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.585
The age range is from you know,
six, seven, eight years old

00:03:23.585 --> 00:03:25.673
to 90 some years old.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.450
There's, as far as we know,
we don't know for a fact

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.660
because we don't really track this,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.560
anybody can really use
Duolingo, but as far as we know

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.953
about 25% of our language
classrooms in the U.S.

00:03:35.953 --> 00:03:38.748
Use Duolingo in one way or another.

00:03:38.748 --> 00:03:41.900
So we do have a lot of student
users but we also have a lot

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.230
of adult users who are just you know,

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.342
wanting to improve on their
high school French or something.

00:03:47.342 --> 00:03:52.342
Since everybody's been staying
at home, we've seen our usage

00:03:52.412 --> 00:03:53.900
go up quite a bit.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.080
And you know, our user
base is pretty global,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.060
only about 20% of our users
are in the United States.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.840
And one thing that's been
interesting is pretty much

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.930
every country, as soon as they apply

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.960
their social distancing
measures, we see traffic increase

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.890
about one or two days after that.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.610
And it depends on the country.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.610
In some countries it
has more than doubled,

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.130
in some countries it has gone
up by 50%, it kinda depends

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.000
on the country, but we've seen that.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.390
The first country obviously
where we saw that was China.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.140
About three months ago,
basically the traffic

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.440
in China doubled and
it's remained like that.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.250
So you know, I don't know what that means.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.819
A lot of China's back to
normal or mostly normal

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:33.118
yet our traffic remains essentially twice

00:04:33.118 --> 00:04:35.177
what it was before.

00:04:35.177 --> 00:04:37.080
- And what do you think
is underlying that?

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.760
Let's say China for example,
I am curious what language

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:40.760
are folks learning?

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.410
Is it all languages, is it English?

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.100
And then what do you
think is the motivation?

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.700
People are like, "I'm
home, I might as well

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.700
do something productive."

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.450
Or is there other motivations?

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.640
- I think there's both.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.250
I think there's a lot of
kids who had to be in school

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.977
and now are home and they're
trying to figure out how to

00:04:59.977 --> 00:05:02.390
you know, how to learn whatever
they used to be learning

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:05.070
and a lot of teachers are
telling them to use Duolingo.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.560
But there's also people
who are just bored at home

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.750
and you know, it used to be
the case that they used to go

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.770
to a restaurant, now that
time is being occupied

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:12.603
for something else.

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:14.192
And they think that you
know, improving their lives

00:05:14.192 --> 00:05:15.700
is an important thing.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.990
So I think a lot of people are doing that.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.088
In terms of what languages
people are learning,

00:05:20.088 --> 00:05:21.629
it really depends on the country.

00:05:21.629 --> 00:05:23.640
In most non-English speaking countries,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:24.920
people are learning English.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.830
That's the main language that
most everybody is learning.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.450
In the United States, the
biggest language is Spanish,

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.410
second is French, then
German, then Japanese.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.668
And then it tapers off quite a bit.

00:05:34.668 --> 00:05:39.668
From English, you can learn
about 35 languages on Duolingo.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.340
But some of the smaller ones,
you can learn High Valyrian

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:47.150
from Game of Thrones, you can
learn you know, Esperanto.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.660
You can learn a lot of kind of
some of the smaller languages

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.381
but not too many people are learning those

00:05:52.381 --> 00:05:54.303
compared to Spanish for example.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.270
- Is High Valyrian a
fully, fleshed out language

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.703
that like we could have
a rich conversation in?

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:01.630
- I think so.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.020
I think there's a lot of
strange things, I think it has

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.900
a lot of different words
for things like "sword"

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:06.916
or stuff like that.

00:06:06.916 --> 00:06:11.107
- Yes, as we know Eskimos have
six words for ice so I guess.

00:06:11.107 --> 00:06:13.790
- Let me tell you something
else which I don't know

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.340
what this says about the world.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.050
When the seasons were
on for Game of Thrones,

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.130
there were more people learning
High Valyrian on Duolingo

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:24.226
than there were people
learning Irish for example

00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:27.530
or Scottish which, yeah, I
don't know what this says

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.990
about the world but that was the case.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.330
- Fascinating, fascinating.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.520
And what's your sense?

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.590
So now there's a lot of parents
and adults who you know,

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.540
I have always had
aspirations to get better

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.540
at certain languages or
learn certain languages.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.920
What's your general advice there?

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.890
I guess this advice would
carry over even if we weren't

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.350
in this crisis situation.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:46.280
Where do you think
Duolingo's really strong

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.651
and then what would you
supplement that with

00:06:48.651 --> 00:06:52.234
if you really were trying to
you know, get conversational

00:06:52.234 --> 00:06:54.793
in Spanish or English
or some other language?

00:06:55.776 --> 00:06:59.127
- Yeah, I mean I think,
well the first thing to know

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:03.960
about learning a language is
it takes a while you know?

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.880
There's a lot of you know,
kind of fake news marketing

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.570
out there that says you can
get fluent in a language

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.620
in nine days or whatever,
this is just not true.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.373
It'll take years to get
really fluent in a language.

00:07:15.373 --> 00:07:17.861
You can get a lot of
progress in a few months

00:07:17.861 --> 00:07:21.360
but if you really, really
wanna get very advanced

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.160
in a language, it takes years.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.360
So the first thing I think
you gotta do is build a habit

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:27.530
as with most everything else.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:31.430
So if you build a habit you
know, spend 15, 20 minutes a day

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.050
using Duolingo and you do it
over a long period of time,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:35.363
you get quite good at it.

00:07:36.677 --> 00:07:40.058
You know, things I would
recommend, once you get to a point

00:07:40.058 --> 00:07:43.210
where you've done about
half the Duolingo course

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.057
or maybe a little more than
that, I would recommend watching

00:07:46.057 --> 00:07:51.057
you know, you can watch
Netflix in you know, Spanish

00:07:52.934 --> 00:07:53.767
or shows in other languages,

00:07:53.767 --> 00:07:55.070
I would recommend watching those.

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.010
A lot of times you can watch
them with the subtitles.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:58.385
Now try watching them with the subtitles

00:07:58.385 --> 00:08:01.660
in the language you're
learning, not in English

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.360
because then you just won't pay attention.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.003
But if you watch with the
subtitles in Spanish too,

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.950
I think that's a pretty good
way to get much better at

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.757
you know, after you do Duolingo.

00:08:12.757 --> 00:08:14.612
But you can't quite start by doing that

00:08:14.612 --> 00:08:17.540
because it's pretty hard
to understand the things.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.100
- Yeah, and I remember
that I used to watch

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:22.505
these Spanish dramas when I
was trying to learn Spanish.

00:08:22.505 --> 00:08:24.060
It is effective.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.690
And you've touched on some questions

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:26.706
you know, from Instagram.

00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:29.597
Sanoff_7 asks, "How do
you establish a habit

00:08:29.597 --> 00:08:30.430
"of learning everyday?"

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:32.300
You just mentioned the
importance of habit.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.433
What do you recommend for
people who are trying that?

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.529
- Yeah, well one of the
important things, I think,

00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:43.400
is to do it everyday at
the same time, that helps.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:47.000
If you just for example,
personally the way I do it

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.667
is basically as soon as I wake up.

00:08:49.667 --> 00:08:53.051
Kinda the first thing
I do is do my Duolingo.

00:08:53.051 --> 00:08:56.197
If you pair it with something
else, it's like, "Well,

00:08:56.197 --> 00:08:58.557
"I'll do it right after I
brush my teeth," or "I'll do it

00:08:58.557 --> 00:09:02.570
"right after lunch," and do
it at the same time everyday,

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.500
that seems to really help.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.659
Another thing is that it's
the first maybe 20 ish days

00:09:07.659 --> 00:09:09.140
that really matter.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.820
I mean, we see it in our data.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.990
If we can get you to
come back consistently

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.090
for about 20 days, then
you'll come back to Duolingo

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.910
for very long periods of time.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.660
So you just gotta try to
stick to it for about 20 days.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.960
- I see, and we have some
questions for you in particular

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:25.810
you know, from YouTube.

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:29.017
Reverend Hogwash is
asking, "How many languages

00:09:29.017 --> 00:09:30.170
"does Luis speak?"

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:31.330
And you just mentioned that you

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.030
are still learning languages,
so I'll extend that.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.060
Not only how many do you
speak but what are you trying

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:36.470
to learn right now?

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.790
- Okay, I will say I am
relatively good at English,

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.978
I am relatively good at
Spanish, about the same,

00:09:41.978 --> 00:09:43.500
English and Spanish.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.253
I'm pretty good at Portuguese,

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.236
not quite as good as English but almost,

00:09:50.236 --> 00:09:55.236
and then I am intermediate
beginner in French.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.650
The pronunciation is
my nemesis for French.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:01.870
- And what are you trying
to learn right now?

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:03.362
You mentioned you still have a habit of--

00:10:03.362 --> 00:10:05.770
- French, I'm doing
French, that's the one.

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.050
I started about a few months
ago, I started with French

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.750
after I learned Portuguese,
I started with French

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:10.600
a few months ago.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.830
- Fascinating, and people
are asking, several folks,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:14.663
this one's from Instagram.

00:10:14.663 --> 00:10:18.063
BakerGirl325 asked, "Why
did you create Duolingo?

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.417
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:24.170
You know, I started
Duolingo, it wasn't just me

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.790
I mean, there's a whole team
of people that works on it

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:27.920
but at the beginning there were two of us,

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.000
me and my cofounder.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.290
His name is Severin and his
last name is Hacker by the way

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:32.733
which is crazy.

00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:38.530
But we started it and the
idea was that we wanted

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:41.970
to do something that
would give equal access

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.260
to education to everybody.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:46.200
I mean, that's something
you know about Sal.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.050
In particular for us, neither
of us is from the U.S.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:52.560
We both had to learn English
when we were growing up.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.930
And English has opened
so many doors for us.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.510
I mean, we were able to
come to the United States,

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:57.343
come to college, etc.

00:10:57.343 --> 00:11:01.169
And we knew that in most
countries in the world,

00:11:01.169 --> 00:11:03.766
non-English speaking
countries of the world,

00:11:03.766 --> 00:11:07.170
knowledge of English can
increase your income potential

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.800
by sometimes as much as 100%.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.110
So you can basically double
your income if you know English.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.122
So we knew that but at the
same time also, the ways

00:11:16.122 --> 00:11:20.090
to learn languages were
pretty expensive at the time.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:23.140
So what we wanted to do was
make a way to learn languages

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.070
that was going to be free.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:28.080
And in particular we wanted
to concentrate on English.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.840
It turns out that you know,
English is a big language

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.410
for us but now we've basically decided

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:34.600
to teach pretty much every language.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.640
We're not there yet,
there are 6,000 languages

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.133
and we only teach 35
but we're working on it.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.210
- And you mentioned
you're free and you know,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:43.970
this is something we have in common.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.480
We have slightly different models,

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:46.650
we're philanthropically funded,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:47.730
but it is an interesting question.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:49.530
I think a couple people
are referring to it.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.110
So how do y'all exist?

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.297
How do you make money if
you're giving it away for free?

00:11:54.297 --> 00:11:57.740
- Yeah, our mission was
always for anybody to be able

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:00.958
to learn a language
entirely free on Duolingo.

00:12:00.958 --> 00:12:01.857
And you know, the question is,

00:12:01.857 --> 00:12:03.070
"How are we gonna make money?"

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:05.780
What we decided to do is
kinda this double thing

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.822
that is similar to example
of how Spotify makes money.

00:12:08.822 --> 00:12:11.960
You can fully learn a language in Duolingo

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:15.530
but after every lesson, you
have to watch a very short ad.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.750
And that's you know,
usually just a static image,

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:19.554
sometimes about you
know, a couple of seconds

00:12:19.554 --> 00:12:21.190
where you have to watch an ad.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.380
So basically watch ads,
that's one way we make money

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.350
but if you don't wanna watch
the ads, you can also pay

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:29.608
to subscribe to Duolingo
and that turns off the ads.

00:12:29.608 --> 00:12:32.293
So it's one of those two ways.

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:37.450
Only 3% of our users pay
us to turn off the ads.

00:12:37.450 --> 00:12:40.193
The other 97% are using
it entirely for free.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.080
- Yeah and you know, this is
an important thing to bring up

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.373
'cause whether you run as a
not-for-profit or a for profit

00:12:46.373 --> 00:12:47.827
there's some way.

00:12:47.827 --> 00:12:50.573
A lot of folks I think
imagine Khan Academy still Sal

00:12:50.573 --> 00:12:53.457
in a closet someplace or they imagine Luis

00:12:53.457 --> 00:12:56.060
and your cofounder Severin Hacker

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:59.695
which is an incredible name
to start a company with

00:12:59.695 --> 00:13:03.410
but you know, it costs a lot of resources.

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.954
And so you have to figure
out some way to make it work

00:13:05.954 --> 00:13:07.560
while still being true to your mission

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.660
which I think y'all are doing a great job.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:12.210
So here's a question, I
don't know if this would fall

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.510
under your domain Luis, but
maybe you have a point of view.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:16.260
I could try to attempt as well.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:17.970
From YouTube, Ashutosh Tiwari,

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.020
who's actually a regular
question asker here,

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:23.627
says, "Hello Luis and Sal,
how do I overcome stuttering

00:13:23.627 --> 00:13:26.810
"if you stutter more in English?"

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.150
I don't know if this is
something that y'all even address

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.350
or do you have a point
of view on this Luis?

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.488
- It's not something
that we do with the app.

00:13:33.488 --> 00:13:37.668
I actually would not know
how to overcome stuttering.

00:13:37.668 --> 00:13:39.385
I myself stutter a little bit.

00:13:39.385 --> 00:13:42.849
So if you have good
ideas Sal, let me know.

00:13:42.849 --> 00:13:44.572
- Yeah, I'm not an expert in it either.

00:13:44.572 --> 00:13:48.110
I have been known to
stutter every now and then.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:49.850
One thing that was
interesting and I don't know

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.360
if this is related to
stuttering but I remember

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.800
in my first job, this was at Oracle,

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:57.670
I was a product manager, and
I had to go to this training

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:58.503
on public speaking.

00:13:58.503 --> 00:14:01.862
And what they did is they video tapped you

00:14:01.862 --> 00:14:03.689
while you gave a speech.

00:14:03.689 --> 00:14:08.689
And then when you watched
it, every time you said "um"

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.130
in your speech, you also had to say "um"

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:13.230
and everyone in the room said "um."

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:15.870
And that made me very
conscientious of "um."

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:17.750
I still say it but you
don't realize how much

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.429
you say words like "um."

00:14:19.429 --> 00:14:23.520
And I know every language
has it's own version of "um."

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.800
- Not only that, so before
this I was a professor

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:31.800
in computer science and on my
first year, somebody told me

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:34.250
that I should video tape
myself in every lecture

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:35.468
and then watch myself.

00:14:35.468 --> 00:14:38.764
First of all, it was
extremely painful and secondly

00:14:38.764 --> 00:14:41.425
you know, you start annotating
things like you know,

00:14:41.425 --> 00:14:43.140
how many times you say "um."

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:47.103
For me, one of the bad things,
I used the word "like" a lot.

00:14:49.566 --> 00:14:50.870
And that was pretty bad.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.520
And I think that actually helped.

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:56.720
- For sure, for sure and
it is a painful experience.

00:14:56.720 --> 00:14:59.320
I try not to even watch
these live streams afterwards

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:00.904
as you can imagine.

00:15:00.904 --> 00:15:05.827
So from YouTube, the Disney
and Travel Guy asks, "Any tips

00:15:05.827 --> 00:15:06.717
"to learn Spanish?

00:15:06.717 --> 00:15:09.287
"I'm trying to learn Spanish
on Duolingo and I really want

00:15:09.287 --> 00:15:10.180
"to be fluent."

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:15.003
So what should the Disney
and Travel Guy do here Luis?

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:17.580
- Well, keep at it.

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:20.710
Like I said, it takes a
while to become fluent

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:21.543
in a language.

00:15:21.543 --> 00:15:26.030
And I would say, once you've
gotten some of the basics

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:29.830
which you know, if you're
maybe down to the middle

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.257
of the Duolingo course,
or three quarters down

00:15:31.257 --> 00:15:34.350
the Duolingo course you
know, I will say something

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.690
that I said before in this.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:38.890
I would try starting watching
some Netflix shows in Spanish.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:39.930
There's some really good ones.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:44.005
I particularly like The Money
Heist but there are others.

00:15:44.005 --> 00:15:47.226
And watch it and if it's too hard for you,

00:15:47.226 --> 00:15:51.119
try maybe children's shows in Spanish,

00:15:51.119 --> 00:15:52.740
they're a little easier.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:57.740
And also try turning on
the subtitling in Spanish.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:00.070
That should help.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.310
- Yep, and what is your
view, for someone who wants

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:06.110
to become fluent, what's
your sense of immersion

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:09.478
or some you know, if you
can't travel to the country,

00:16:09.478 --> 00:16:13.006
some version of immersion
like getting on video chats

00:16:13.006 --> 00:16:15.342
with folks or something like that?

00:16:15.342 --> 00:16:16.890
What role does that play?

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:17.723
- All of that helps.

00:16:17.723 --> 00:16:22.723
There's a problem with it, it
is most people have too much

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:26.070
social anxiety to get on video
chats with random strangers

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:28.650
in a language that
they're not very good at.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:30.706
So you know, if you're
able to overcome that,

00:16:30.706 --> 00:16:32.750
that's a pretty good solution.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.979
But I think a lot of
people, it's just too much

00:16:34.979 --> 00:16:37.136
social pressure for them.

00:16:37.136 --> 00:16:40.555
So in that case you know, I don't think

00:16:40.555 --> 00:16:42.710
that's absolutely necessary.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.570
I think you can get pretty
far by just using tools

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.607
like Duolingo and you
know, watching TV shows

00:16:48.607 --> 00:16:50.420
and stuff like that.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.700
- Cool, so if you can obviously Duolingo,

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:56.020
the Netflix in Spanish or
whatever, those are great things

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:58.140
that anyone can do like starting now.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:01.282
And then if you can overcome
the social pressure,

00:17:01.282 --> 00:17:03.480
maybe get on some of--

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.840
I don't know their names but
there's ways that you can

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:10.060
get connected with folks and
you know, talk to live people

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.090
in the other languages.

00:17:11.090 --> 00:17:12.640
So it's interesting.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:15.318
So this is a question
I guess for both of us

00:17:15.318 --> 00:17:20.318
from Instagram, Manfree.ganmore
asks, "Does the speed

00:17:21.117 --> 00:17:23.727
"at which you learn affect
the retention of knowledge

00:17:23.727 --> 00:17:25.140
"for language or math?"

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.000
So you can take the language
side, or if you have

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.650
a point of view on the math side.

00:17:29.643 --> 00:17:31.041
Do you think that
certain people are better

00:17:31.041 --> 00:17:32.823
at languages than others
or do you think it's just

00:17:32.823 --> 00:17:35.482
in our minds?

00:17:35.482 --> 00:17:39.052
- You know, I do think
there are certain people

00:17:39.052 --> 00:17:40.904
who are a little better than others.

00:17:40.904 --> 00:17:43.762
There's been research about that.

00:17:43.762 --> 00:17:45.650
You know, some of it has to
do with general intelligence

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:50.650
but actually, one of the biggest
things is just being okay

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:54.248
sounding not so smart.

00:17:54.248 --> 00:17:57.210
If you're okay with that,
I think you're better

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.043
at learning languages.

00:17:58.043 --> 00:18:00.489
Because one of the biggest
things that helps you

00:18:00.489 --> 00:18:04.065
learn languages faster
is if you start speaking,

00:18:04.065 --> 00:18:07.850
the sooner you start
basically able to vocalize

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.090
and say stuff, the better.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:10.950
This is why with Duolingo we recommend

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:12.470
that whenever you're doing
the Duolingo exercises

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:14.790
you just try to mouth them,
just like kinda vocalize

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:15.727
everything that you're seeing.

00:18:15.727 --> 00:18:19.358
The sooner you start being
able to talk to others,

00:18:19.358 --> 00:18:20.973
the better you get.

00:18:20.973 --> 00:18:23.575
And so one of the ways in
which some people are better

00:18:23.575 --> 00:18:27.860
at learning languages is they
just basically don't care

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:31.163
as much about sounding you know, broken.

00:18:32.639 --> 00:18:35.439
- What you're really saying
is you know, we talk a lot

00:18:35.439 --> 00:18:38.070
about things like mindset
and growth mindset

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:39.610
here at Khan academy
and you're really making

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:40.443
the same point.

00:18:40.443 --> 00:18:42.781
That people who are more
comfortable putting themselves

00:18:42.781 --> 00:18:46.003
out there, they're not
afraid to fail, those folks

00:18:46.003 --> 00:18:48.497
in general are gonna grow
more versus the people

00:18:48.497 --> 00:18:50.347
who are like, "Oh, I
don't sound intelligent

00:18:50.347 --> 00:18:54.051
"in this language yet, let me
just kind of keep preparing."

00:18:54.051 --> 00:18:54.884
- Yes.

00:18:54.884 --> 00:18:56.717
- And I think the exact
same thing is in math.

00:18:56.717 --> 00:19:00.380
You know, I think people are
quick to label themselves

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:02.770
or others are slow
learners or fast learners.

00:19:02.770 --> 00:19:05.287
You might see the kid in your
class and she's always getting

00:19:05.287 --> 00:19:07.517
the As and you're always
getting a few points less.

00:19:07.517 --> 00:19:08.656
"Oh, maybe she's a fast learner."

00:19:08.656 --> 00:19:12.043
But the reality is, she
was probably the person

00:19:12.043 --> 00:19:14.448
who's always pushing, stepping
out of her comfort zone

00:19:14.448 --> 00:19:16.600
willing to get questions wrong.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.721
If she gets the question
wrong, she'll reflect on it.

00:19:18.721 --> 00:19:22.340
And she's always trying to draw
connections between things.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.630
I think probably the same
thing is true in languages

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:26.467
as in math, is the more
associations you can make

00:19:26.467 --> 00:19:30.410
with something, the more
it'll be fluent to you,

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:32.280
the more it'll come to your--

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.220
You know, I'm not a expert
at this but at least

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.790
I'm experienced in observing others.

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:38.320
And we've interfaced
with a lot of the experts

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.095
on things like grit and growth mindset.

00:19:40.095 --> 00:19:43.673
That really seems to
be the best correlate.

00:19:44.892 --> 00:19:45.955
- Totally agree.

00:19:45.955 --> 00:19:50.769
- So let's see, from Instagram,
SamI804 asks, "How can I

00:19:50.769 --> 00:19:54.250
"make the process of
learning a language fun?"

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:57.394
And I'm assuming above and
beyond using the fun Duolingo.

00:19:57.394 --> 00:20:00.752
Or how can I make using
Duolingo even more fun?

00:20:00.752 --> 00:20:02.710
- The main way is by using Duolingo.

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:05.860
I mean, we spend a lot
of effort making Duolingo

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:06.945
as fun as possible.

00:20:06.945 --> 00:20:09.800
There's a lot of features in
Duolingo that make it more fun.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.990
For example, we have these
leader boards with leagues

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:14.790
where the idea is that
whenever you join Duolingo,

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:19.790
you get grouped with about
50 other people who started

00:20:20.826 --> 00:20:22.827
around the same time as you.

00:20:22.827 --> 00:20:25.634
And for the next week,
you get to see who gets

00:20:25.634 --> 00:20:27.840
more experience points.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.890
And the top 10 people in
that group of 50 move on

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:33.200
to the next league.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:35.490
So everybody starts in the bronze league,

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:37.850
then the top 10 move on
to the silver league,

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:41.040
then next week the top 10
move on to the gold league.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.440
And we have a bunch of leagues like that.

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:45.190
So that competition actually makes the app

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:46.583
significantly more fun.

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:49.330
So I think that's one thing.

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:54.330
Another thing that we
really believe in is that

00:20:54.557 --> 00:20:56.430
with Duolingo you can learn you know,

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:58.320
in two to three minute segments.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.490
So anytime you basically,
you are waiting for something

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:03.070
for two minutes or something
you can just get in

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:03.903
a little bit of Duolingo.

00:21:03.903 --> 00:21:06.237
And I think that's another
way if you just parse it out

00:21:06.237 --> 00:21:09.423
into smaller chunks, that helps.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:11.313
- That's great.

00:21:11.313 --> 00:21:13.540
And on Facebook, a question
from Margo McGinness.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:14.691
Oh hi Margo, I know Margo.

00:21:14.691 --> 00:21:17.967
"My kids need to catch up
with sixth grade Spanish

00:21:17.967 --> 00:21:19.987
"for a new school over the summer starting

00:21:19.987 --> 00:21:22.247
"from scratch, any tips?"

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:26.633
- Well, download Duolingo.

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:33.280
For sure download Duolingo.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:39.850
You know, other tips
of things that we hear

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.180
other than Duolingo and
maybe watching some Netflix

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.490
in Spanish is try reading
the news in Spanish.

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:48.790
There's also some podcasts.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:50.300
By the way, we have a podcast,

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:52.338
there's the Duolingo Spanish
podcast but there's others

00:21:52.338 --> 00:21:54.730
and you can try to
listen to some podcasts.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.788
Some of them are pretty fun.

00:21:56.788 --> 00:21:58.810
The Duolingo in particular
one, the Duolingo podcast

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:03.539
is basically real stories
of people from Latin America

00:22:03.539 --> 00:22:06.510
about stuff you know, like soccer players

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.260
and stuff like that, that
are in very slow Spanish.

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:10.513
And that helps you.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.950
- Yeah, well one of my ideas
just speaking generally

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:18.101
about learning languages, I
have memories of you know,

00:22:18.101 --> 00:22:23.101
my family's original tongue is
Bangali but my mom also spoke

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:27.300
you know, Hindi/Urdu
to some of her friends.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:28.640
And you know, growing up in New Orleans,

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.180
there's no immersion for that.

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:33.160
But I would hear her
conversations with her friends

00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:35.199
and especially when she was
saying something really juicy

00:22:35.199 --> 00:22:38.250
that wasn't meant for my
ears, it would be either

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:43.234
in Bengali, Hindi/Urdu and
that's how I got my functional--

00:22:43.234 --> 00:22:45.883
In your brain, it definitely picks up.

00:22:45.883 --> 00:22:47.380
- A high reward to understanding.

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:48.360
You got to understand the juicy stuff.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.100
- Right, right, you're
brain wants to understand

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:53.183
what it's not allowed to understand.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:56.860
But yeah, so yeah, I can imagine
actually Margo's situation.

00:22:56.860 --> 00:23:00.340
A lot of parents are trying
to figure out you know,

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:02.240
it might be a situation like
that that you're transitioning

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:06.500
a school or obviously school's
out, can you leverage?

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:07.950
You know, we've been
advocating how you can use

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:09.830
the remainder of the school
year and even the summer

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:13.070
to keep learning in math
but obviously the same thing

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:17.450
can happen in Spanish or
languages or other subjects.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:21.163
So other questions here.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:24.277
A few people are asking Luis,

00:23:24.277 --> 00:23:26.915
"What was your original tongue?"

00:23:26.915 --> 00:23:28.754
Spanish I'm assuming.

00:23:28.754 --> 00:23:30.773
- I am a native Spanish speaker, yes.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:33.520
- Where did you grow up?

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:38.520
- I grew up in Guatemala,
yes right underneath Mexico,

00:23:38.673 --> 00:23:39.727
that's where it is.

00:23:39.727 --> 00:23:41.780
- And so these other
languages that you've learned,

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:45.070
are learning, like Portuguese
and French, this is just out

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:47.316
of interest or do you have a
lot of friends who travel a lot

00:23:47.316 --> 00:23:49.436
to those regions?

00:23:49.436 --> 00:23:50.269
- It's out of interest.

00:23:50.269 --> 00:23:53.080
I mean you know, I figured I
should learn some languages

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:55.053
given that I am the CEO of Duolingo.

00:23:55.925 --> 00:23:59.423
So yes, but it is out of
interest in those languages.

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:01.810
- Right, well great.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.890
Well, let's see, you've
covered a lot of this.

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:06.851
Scott asks, "How many languages
do you offer to learn?"

00:24:06.851 --> 00:24:09.380
And I think you said, what was it 35?

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:11.963
- 35, from English, you
can learn 35 languages.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:15.810
- Yep, and here's a question from YouTube.

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.657
Nikhil Govender asks, "If someone has

00:24:17.657 --> 00:24:20.410
"a learning disability,"
they're asking both Khan Academy

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:21.870
and Duolingo how it's suited to help.

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:23.173
Any thoughts there Luis?

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:27.180
- Well, depends on what
learning disability.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.870
But I think we know of a lot
of people who have all kinds

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:32.050
of different learning disabilities

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:33.800
who very successfully use Duolingo.

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:40.783
You know, we try to make the
app as accessible as possible.

00:24:42.471 --> 00:24:44.694
And yeah, for example we know
a lot of people with dyslexia

00:24:44.694 --> 00:24:46.250
that successfully use Duolingo.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.060
You can turn off certain exercise types

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:50.510
and that seems to help.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.200
So depending on the learning disability

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.253
I think it can work pretty well.

00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:57.665
- Yeah, we see a similar
situation on the Khan Academy side

00:24:57.665 --> 00:25:00.851
that we haven't done any focused studies

00:25:00.851 --> 00:25:03.760
for many learning disabilities
but to your point,

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:05.870
we're trying to make it
as accessible as possible.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.957
And then we've anecdotally heard
from certain students maybe

00:25:08.957 --> 00:25:12.451
with ADHD or dyslexia or some place

00:25:12.451 --> 00:25:15.784
on the Asperger's spectrum,
in certain cases you know,

00:25:15.784 --> 00:25:18.690
being able to work at your own pace,

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:20.420
slow down things, speed things up,

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:22.660
get immediate feedback on
exercises, things like that

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:25.770
which you know, is true
of both of our platforms

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.261
and other online platforms,
that that sometimes

00:25:28.261 --> 00:25:29.960
can be helpful.

00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:32.860
But I think there's probably
a lot more research to be had.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:37.100
Well Luis, thanks for joining us

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:39.750
and answering these
questions about language.

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:41.970
I think this is an area
where you know, Khan Academy,

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:43.657
I get a lot of questions,
"When is Khan Academy going

00:25:43.657 --> 00:25:44.930
"to teach language?"

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:47.780
And you know, we have Khan
Academy in other languages

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:49.610
but I always say, there's
no need for us to do it

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:50.860
because there's Duolingo.

00:25:52.554 --> 00:25:53.387
- Well thank you.

00:25:53.387 --> 00:25:55.510
- So go to Duolingo and figure out--

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:58.010
So you know, keep doing
the amazing work y'all do.

00:25:58.960 --> 00:25:59.793
- No, and thank you.

00:25:59.793 --> 00:26:02.780
And thank you for doing this
and for all the amazing work.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:05.175
We're huge fans of Khan Academy here.

00:26:05.175 --> 00:26:07.256
- Likewise.

00:26:07.256 --> 00:26:08.354
- Thank you.

00:26:08.354 --> 00:26:13.354
- So everyone, there's a
couple of other questions

00:26:13.873 --> 00:26:17.520
that y'all are asking
generally but I will just say

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:18.960
you know, we're doing this everyday

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.690
and I think we're coming
up to the half hour.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:23.190
But you know, keep your questions going.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:27.380
If I can't get them in over
today or over the next few days,

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:29.667
then I will try to answer
them in future days.

00:26:29.667 --> 00:26:32.730
But as I mentioned, this is
just a forum for all of us

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:35.370
to stay in touch, stay connected
during the school closures,

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:38.280
talk about things that might
be relevant for getting you

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:39.390
through the school closures

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:41.300
or things that might just be interesting

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:43.070
since we're all socially distanced now.

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:45.930
Expect over the next few
weeks, we will have many other

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.900
interesting guests like
Luis to talk about anything

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:51.084
from how you might try to
tackle a certain subject,

00:26:51.084 --> 00:26:54.750
how you might try to stay
just happy and content

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:56.900
in this otherwise stressful period

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:59.100
or things about how you
can be creative at home

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.750
or what's the state of the economy?

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:05.000
I'll also put my plug in
